EU environment ministers will exchange  views on way to tackle illegal shipments  of waste that endanger human health and  the environment in talks in Luxembourg  on October 14 around twenty five percent  of waste shipments enter Asia or Africa  from the EU are thought to contravene  international standards the waste is  also often carelessly handled or dumped  when it arrives The ministers will  debate how the EU might implement new  proposals including spot inspections  better training of inspectors and  requirements for suspects to provide  evidence that any way shipment complies  with the law the way whether the  proposals are flexible enough and fair  for all stakeholders standards on waste  shipments vary widely in Europe and  exporters of illegal waste tend to move  it from countries where the controls are  most lenient the ministers are also said  to discuss the UN climate change  conference in Warsaw next month the EU  is a major international actor in the  fight against global warming The  ministers will set out the main elements  of the EU stance on the climate talks  which run from the 11th to the 22nd of  November they'll focus on the proper  implementation of existing decisions and  on the road map to guide countries  toward a legal deal to cut carbon  emissions in 2015 which would enter  force after 2020 economy and finance  ministers will also discuss the climate  change conference when they meet the  following day also up for debate on the  agenda at the environment Council is a  compromise text to help the EU meet its  target for cutting carbon dioxide  emissions from new passenger cars the  union wants to reduce greenhouse gases  from new cars by eighty to ninety five  percent by 2050 compared with levels  from 1990 current policies are only  likely to result in a forty percent  reduction by then
 

